            <ArticleTitle>Underrecognized postdosing reactions to methotrexate in patients with rheumatoid arthritis.</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>1224-6</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="OBJECTIVE" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To report previously underrecognized postdosing side effects of methotrexate (MTX).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">Over a one and a half year period, in 2 practices, all patients with rheumatoid arthritis (RA) receiving weekly MTX, and volunteering symptoms within a few hours of MTX dosing were carefully questioned concerning the nature of the postdosing reaction.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Of 356 patients, 36 (10%) reported a previously underemphasized postdosing reaction consisting of arthralgias/myalgias, fatigue/malaise or both. Sixteen patients discontinued MTX primarily because of this side effect.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSION" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">In these patient populations, postdosing reactions were the 2nd most common side effect (after gastrointestinal) leading to MTX withdrawal.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
